1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,963
How Memryoshka works with a vision-language model.

2
00:00:04,263 --> 00:00:08,628
Each incoming frame goes through the model's own vision encoder,

3
00:00:08,478 --> 00:00:12,712
which turns it into about two hundred and fifty visual tokens.

4
00:00:13,012 --> 00:00:17,099
The encoder's attention marks the tokens that matter most,

5
00:00:16,949 --> 00:00:20,832
and a small CLIP model gives each frame one search tag.

6
00:00:21,132 --> 00:00:26,200
Each token is projected onto five hundred and twelve fixed directions,

7
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:29,431
quantized, and written as a nested bit stream.

8
00:00:29,731 --> 00:00:31,901
The memory has a fixed size.

9
00:00:31,751 --> 00:00:38,825
When it fills up, one price per bit shortens codes and drops the least useful tokens and frames.

10
00:00:39,125 --> 00:00:42,884
When a question arrives, it is matched against the search tags,

11
00:00:42,734 --> 00:00:46,207
and the best-matching frames are decoded from their codes.

12
00:00:46,507 --> 00:00:50,158
The decoded tokens go back to their places, with timestamps,

13
00:00:50,008 --> 00:00:53,601
and the frozen language model reads them with the question.

14
00:00:53,901 --> 00:00:55,344
Nothing is trained.

15
00:00:55,194 --> 00:01:00,514
The vision encoder, the language model and the search model all stay frozen.
